WEBVTT

NOTE week05-clip3-preference-tuning
NOTE Narration read by an AI-generated voice.
NOTE The clip has its own caption along the bottom of the frame, so these cues
NOTE are placed near the top to keep the two from overlapping.

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:05.122 line:2 align:center
Preference data pairs one
question with two answers.

2
00:00:05.122 --> 00:00:09.512 line:2 align:center
A clinician marks one as chosen
and the other as rejected.

3
00:00:09.512 --> 00:00:14.483 line:2 align:center
Here the rejected answer
gets the negation wrong.

4
00:00:14.683 --> 00:00:20.297 line:2 align:center
Right now, the small Qwen3 model rates
the wrong answer as far more likely.

5
00:00:20.297 --> 00:00:23.888 line:2 align:center
Its log-probability is minus 1.12,

6
00:00:23.888 --> 00:00:28.283 line:2 align:center
while the correct answer gets minus 21.37.

7
00:00:28.483 --> 00:00:30.233 line:2 align:center
The classic method is RLHF.

8
00:00:30.233 --> 00:00:36.564 line:2 align:center
First, a separate reward model learns
to score answers from many such choices.

9
00:00:36.564 --> 00:00:38.571 line:2 align:center
Then reinforcement learning,

10
00:00:38.571 --> 00:00:43.345 line:2 align:center
usually PPO, updates the language
model to earn high scores.

11
00:00:43.345 --> 00:00:49.550 line:2 align:center
That means two models to train,
and the training can be unstable.

12
00:00:49.750 --> 00:00:54.718 line:2 align:center
DPO skips the reward model. It keeps
a frozen copy of the starting model,

13
00:00:54.718 --> 00:00:57.372 line:2 align:center
and for each answer it measures a gain:

14
00:00:57.372 --> 00:01:00.577 line:2 align:center
how much more likely the
model in training makes

15
00:01:00.577 --> 00:01:03.566 line:2 align:center
that answer than the frozen copy does.

16
00:01:03.566 --> 00:01:08.150 line:2 align:center
The frozen copy keeps the model
close to what it already knew.

17
00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:12.555 line:2 align:center
The DPO loss takes the chosen
gain minus the rejected gain,

18
00:01:12.555 --> 00:01:19.463 line:2 align:center
scales it by a small number called beta,
here 0.1, and passes it through a sigmoid.

19
00:01:19.463 --> 00:01:23.817 line:2 align:center
The loss gets smaller as
the chosen gain pulls ahead.

20
00:01:24.017 --> 00:01:30.213 line:2 align:center
At the start, the model is identical to
its frozen copy, so both gains are zero.

21
00:01:30.213 --> 00:01:35.831 line:2 align:center
The sigmoid of 0 is one half, and the
loss is minus the log of one half,

22
00:01:35.831 --> 00:01:38.683 line:2 align:center
which is 0.69.

23
00:01:38.883 --> 00:01:42.113 line:2 align:center
After some training,

24
00:01:42.113 --> 00:01:45.518 line:2 align:center
the chosen answer has gained four
and the rejected answer has lost six.

25
00:01:45.518 --> 00:01:51.903 line:2 align:center
Beta times the gap of 10 is
1, the sigmoid of 1 is 0.73,

26
00:01:51.903 --> 00:01:55.947 line:2 align:center
and the loss falls to 0.31.
